# keyvault.conf — what `keyvault pack` collects.
#
# `keyvault init` copies this to ~/.config/keyvault/keyvault.conf. That copy is yours:
# it names your keys and where they live, so it never belongs in a repository.
#
# The file is plain bash that keyvault sources, so quoting, $HOME, variables and comments
# all behave the way you expect. Nothing in it is secret: account names, paths and public
# keys only. The keys themselves are never written here.
#
# Directives:
#
#   level <biometric|passphrase|both>
#       What it takes to open everything declared after this line (default: biometric).
#       Any source can also take its own --level. Every level also opens with the
#       recovery key from `keyvault setup`.
#         biometric    Touch ID
#         passphrase   the passphrase you type (an agent cannot)
#         both         Touch ID and the passphrase
#
#   dest <path>
#       Where the encrypted vault, its archive and catalog.json live. Somewhere that
#       syncs is the point: the vault is useless without its keys, so it can go anywhere.
#       Default: ~/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Keyvault
#
#   sparkle <keychain-account> [--plist PATH] [--desc TEXT] [--level L]
#       A Sparkle ed25519 update-signing key from the login keychain. --plist points at
#       the Info.plist that ships the matching SUPublicEDKey; `keyvault validate` then
#       proves the backed-up key still signs updates installed copies will accept.
#
#   identities <keychain> [--desc TEXT] [--level L]
#       Every code-signing identity (certificate + private key) in a keychain, exported
#       as one .p12. macOS asks once per key — click Allow.
#
#   file <path> [--id NAME] [--desc TEXT] [--level L]
#       One file, restored to the same path with the same mode.
#
#   glob <dir> <pattern> [--desc TEXT] [--level L]
#       Every file in <dir> matching <pattern>. For folders that grow: new keys are
#       picked up without editing this file, and `validate` tells you when one appears.
#
#   meta <id> key=value …
#       Facts an agent needs to *use* an item without seeing it — an App Store Connect
#       issuer id, which app a key serves. Published in catalog.json. Key ids, certificate
#       subjects and expiry, and public keys are derived on their own.
#
# Anything added with `keyvault add` is carried across repacks and needs no line here.
#
# A good question for every line: can this be re-issued in an afternoon? If yes, it
# probably does not belong here — a backup of a revocable token widens the blast radius
# more than it saves you. Write down what you left out, and why, as comments.

# dest "$HOME/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Keyvault"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- cannot be re-issued
#
# Lose a Sparkle key and no installed copy of the app can be updated again.

# sparkle my-app --level both \
#     --plist "$HOME/src/my-app/Resources/Info.plist" \
#     --desc "MyApp Sparkle update-signing key."

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- painful to re-issue

# identities login.keychain-db --level both \
#     --desc "Developer ID and Apple Distribution certificates with their private keys."

# App Store Connect API keys can be downloaded exactly once, at creation.
# glob "$HOME/.appstoreconnect/private_keys" '*.p8' \
#     --desc "App Store Connect API keys."

# GitHub App private keys are shown once at generation.
# file "$HOME/Documents/my-github-app.private-key.pem" \
#     --id github-app-my-app --desc "GitHub App private key for my-app."

# A SOPS/age master key decrypts every secret the project ever encrypted.
# file "$HOME/.config/sops/age/keys.txt" --id sops-age --level both --desc "SOPS age master key."

# Recovery codes and one-off secrets go in with `keyvault add`, not here:
#   keyvault add github-recovery-codes --file ~/Downloads/github-recovery-codes.txt --level both

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- for agents
#
# Issuer ids: appstoreconnect.apple.com → Users and Access → Integrations.
#
# meta AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8 issuer_id=<uuid> used_by=my-app
# meta sparkle-my-app app=MyApp
